---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Wargame Modules · Ray Weiss"
  description="Commissioned digital modules of print wargames: rules-enforced engines, real maps and counters, a computer opponent in the price, playable in a browser from a double-click. $400 to $800 by scope."
>
  <div
    slot="sheet-id"
    class="sheet-id"
    aria-label="Sheet identification"
  >
    <div class="plate">
      <span class="num">PL. I.C</span> Wargame Modules · Commissions Open
    </div>
    <div class="edition">Edition 2026.10 · Brooklyn, NY</div>
  </div>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
    <header class="hero">
      <img
        src="/counter-rw.svg"
        alt="Ray Weiss unit counter"
        class="counter"
        width="96"
        height="72"
      />
      <div>
        <h1>Wargame Modules</h1>
        <p class="dek">Print wargames. Running locally in a browser.</p>
        <div class="slug" aria-label="Identification">
          <span><strong>UNIT</strong> RW / 26</span>
          <span class="sep">/</span>
          <span><strong>TYPE</strong> Digital modules</span>
          <span class="sep">/</span>
          <span><strong>STATUS</strong> Open, selective</span>
          <span class="sep">/</span>
          <span><strong>SCALE</strong> $400 to monster</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bio">
      <p>
        I build digital modules of print wargames on commission. The map, the counters, and the
        procedures are translated into a local engine. You unzip a folder and double-click
        index.html. It runs in your browser. No installs, no accounts, no internet connection
        required. If you have no one to play, the module can play the other side.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section aria-labelledby="module-includes">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 class="section-label" id="module-includes">
          <span class="pl">PL. I.C</span>What a module includes
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>
          Automated bookkeeping and chart lookups. Hotseat play with information masking for cards
          and hidden movement. Every roll logs the cited rule. Games save to local files. The
          price includes a computer opponent that can take either side.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img
          src="/images/wargame-modules/nab-crop.webp"
          alt="Napoleon at Bay digital module. Hex map of the Champagne countryside with marshal counters, and a turn control panel showing weather, morale, and the segment sequence."
          width="800"
          class="w-full rounded"
          loading="eager"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          Napoleon at Bay. Turn control, weather, morale, and the segment sequence on the digitized
          map.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img
          src="/images/wargame-modules/flattop-crop.webp"
          alt="Flattop digital module. Search-board hexes over the Solomons with an umpire traffic panel reporting air operations by teletype."
          width="800"
          class="w-full rounded"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          Flattop. The umpire reports contacts by teletype. Hidden movement stays hidden.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img
          src="/images/wargame-modules/pog-crop.webp"
          alt="Paths of Glory digital module. The Central Powers card hand beside the eastern map edge, each card offering operations or event play."
          width="800"
          class="w-full rounded"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          Paths of Glory. The Central Powers hand, each card playable for ops or event through the
          engine.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img
          src="/images/wargame-modules/ftp-crop.webp"
          alt="For the People digital module. Point-to-point Civil War map with armies and forts, and the Confederate hand of four strategy cards beside it."
          width="800"
          class="w-full rounded"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          For the People. The point-to-point map with armies, forts, and the Confederate hand.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="client-word" class="mt-16">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 class="section-label" id="client-word">
          <span class="pl">PL. I.C</span>From a client
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>
          Bloody April is GMT's game of the 1917 air war over Arras: flights of aircraft at
          different altitudes, sighting rules, and movement unlike a standard hex-and-counter game.
          David Greenwood commissioned the module and a computer opponent that plays either side.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img
          src="/images/wargame-modules/bloody-april-board.webp"
          alt="Bloody April digital module. Operational map of the Arras front with German flights and British contacts, a flight-movement panel showing altitude, and the sequence of play."
          width="800"
          class="w-full rounded"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          Bloody April. Flights, altitude, and the sequence of play on the Arras map.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="mt-8">
        <img
          src="/images/wargame-modules/bloody-april-ai.webp"
          alt="Bloody April digital module. Operations log of computer-opponent decisions and a resolved air combat, each entry citing its rule section."
          width="800"
          class="w-full rounded"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          Bloody April. The computer opponent's moves and a resolved air combat in the phase record, with rule citations.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="prose-body mt-8">
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            I highly recommend Ray's work at developing AI opponents for wargames. I presented him
            with the challenge of Bloody April, a WWI operational air war game. This game is quite
            complex, and while it is a hex and counter game, it is very different from standard hex
            and counter games. It includes multilevel flight of groups of planes, sighting rules and
            unusual movement rules. In order to create an AI, Ray had to first digitize the game. He
            took it on enthusiastically, and the result is I can now play a favorite game that I have
            had trouble finding human opponents for. The AI is a challenging opponent and can play
            either side.
          </p>
          <p>
            Working with Ray has been great. He gave frequent updates, was open to suggestions and
            requests, and was very accommodating. He developed an innovative approach to testing,
            using a panel of AI developers to simulate games and then rate the results. This
            enabled him to go through cycles of development and testing in a very short amount of
            time.
          </p>
          <p>
            This was a VERY complex project due to its atypical rules. If someone wants a standard
            SPI-like or other standard hex and counter ground wargame, he can complete it within
            days. Either way, given what it would cost to have a developer do similar work, it is a
            bargain.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <div class="font-mono text-xs text-subtle">David Greenwood, Bloody April commission</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="track-record" class="mt-16">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 class="section-label" id="track-record">
          <span class="pl">PL. I.C</span>Track record
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>
          Seven commissions for four clients since July 2026: Napoleon at Bay, Flattop, Paths of
          Glory, For the People, Bloody April, and two private tabletop builds. The first client
          ordered a second pair after testing the first two. Card-driven engines pass six hundred
          automated rules checks before delivery. Playable previews arrive within two days of
          commission.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="demo" class="mt-16">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 class="section-label" id="demo">
          <span class="pl">PL. I.C</span>Try one
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>
          The World Undone is my own published design through Conflict Simulations. Its Deluxe
          Digital edition, on itch.io, covers East Prussia, Galicia, Serbia, and the combined East
          Front campaign, hotseat or against the computer. The free East Prussia playtest build is
          the same product class I deliver for commissions: a rules-enforced engine, hex map,
          counters, and hotseat play. Open it in your browser and play.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <a href="https://conflict-simulations-llc.itch.io/the-world-undone" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <img
            src="/images/wargame-modules/twu-deluxe.webp"
            alt="The World Undone Deluxe Digital, Galicia campaign, turn 9. Hex map with Russian and Austro-Hungarian counters, the turn track, victory points, and the combat results table."
            width="800"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </a>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          <a href="https://conflict-simulations-llc.itch.io/the-world-undone" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            The World Undone: East Front Trilogy, Deluxe Digital on itch.io &rarr;
          </a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="mt-8">
        <a href="https://twu-ep-pt-dba90a.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <img
            src="/images/wargame-modules/twu-demo.webp"
            alt="1914: East Prussia: The World Undone in play. Full hex map of East Prussia with German and Russian counters, turn track, victory point track, and combat results table."
            width="800"
            class="w-full rounded"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </a>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-subtle">
          <a href="https://twu-ep-pt-dba90a.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Play the East Prussia 1914 playtest &rarr;
          </a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="pricing" class="mt-16">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 class="section-label" id="pricing">
          <span class="pl">PL. I.C</span>Pricing
        </h2>
        <span class="section-count">04 tiers ——</span>
      </div>
      <ol class="infra-list">
        <li>
          <div class="infra-meta">
            <span><span class="k">Price</span><span class="v">$400</span></span>
            <span><span class="k">Scope</span><span class="v">Smaller games</span></span>
          </div>
          <span class="infra-title">Single map, limited counter mix</span>
          <p class="infra-desc">Chart-driven procedures. No card deck.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="infra-meta">
            <span><span class="k">Price</span><span class="v">$600</span></span>
            <span><span class="k">Scope</span><span class="v">Standard operational</span></span>
          </div>
          <span class="infra-title">Full map, card deck or comparable subsystems</span>
          <p class="infra-desc">The Napoleon at Bay class.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="infra-meta">
            <span><span class="k">Price</span><span class="v">$800</span></span>
            <span><span class="k">Scope</span><span class="v">Complex</span></span>
          </div>
          <span class="infra-title">Hidden information, complicated mapping, layered mechanics</span>
          <p class="infra-desc">The Flattop, Paths of Glory, and For the People class: double-blind umpiring, extensive decks, dense hexside and connection work.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="infra-meta">
            <span><span class="k">Price</span><span class="v">Quoted</span></span>
            <span><span class="k">Scope</span><span class="v">Monster games</span></span>
          </div>
          <span class="infra-title">Monster games quoted per project</span>
          <p class="infra-desc">Multiple maps, thousands of counters, campaign scale.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="prose-body mt-6">
        <p>
          You pay half up front to reserve the build spot, half on completion. Thirty days of
          reasonable feature requests and bug fixes after delivery are included.
        </p>
        <p>
          These are private builds for owners of print copies. Publishers may commission licensed
          editions under separate terms, including translated editions built from the same engine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="hex-rule" aria-hidden="true">
      <img src="/hex-ornament.svg" alt="" width="96" height="32" />
    </div>

    <aside class="featured" aria-labelledby="start">
      <span class="section-label" id="start">Start</span>
      <h3>One email starts it</h3>
      <p class="desc">
        Email <a href="mailto:rweiss@consimsltd.com">rweiss@consimsltd.com</a>. Provide the game
        title, edition, and any specific technical requirements. If the work fits, I reply with
        scope and price. If it does not, I say so.
      </p>
      <a href="mailto:rweiss@consimsltd.com" class="cta">Email me &rarr;</a>
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
